NNOOTTEESS
       A process can create multiple signalfd file descriptors.  This makes it
       possible  to  accept  different  signals on different file descriptors.
       (This may be useful if monitoring the file descriptors using sseelleecctt(2),
       ppoollll(2), or eeppoollll(7): the arrival of different signals will  make  dif‐
       ferent  file  descriptors  ready.)   If a signal appears in the _m_a_s_k of
       more than one of the file descriptors, then occurrences of that  signal
       can be read (once) from any one of the file descriptors.

       Attempts to include SSIIGGKKIILLLL and SSIIGGSSTTOOPP in _m_a_s_k are silently ignored.

       The  signal  mask  employed by a signalfd file descriptor can be viewed
       via the entry for the corresponding file descriptor  in  the  process’s
       _/_p_r_o_c_/pid_/_f_d_i_n_f_o directory.  See pprroocc(5) for further details.

   LLiimmiittaattiioonnss
       The  signalfd  mechanism can’t be used to receive signals that are syn‐
       chronously generated, such as the SSIIGGSSEEGGVV signal that results from  ac‐
       cessing  an  invalid  memory  address or the SSIIGGFFPPEE signal that results
       from an arithmetic error.  Such signals can be caught only  via  signal
       handler.

       As described above, in normal usage one blocks the signals that will be
       accepted  via  ssiiggnnaallffdd().   If  spawning  a child process to execute a
       helper program (that does not need the signalfd file descriptor), then,
       after the call to ffoorrkk(2), you will normally want to unblock those sig‐
       nals before calling eexxeeccvvee(2), so that the helper program can  see  any
       signals  that it expects to see.  Be aware, however, that this won’t be
       possible in the case of a helper program spawned behind the  scenes  by
       any  library  function  that  the program may call.  In such cases, one
       must fall back to using a traditional signal handler that writes  to  a
       file descriptor monitored by sseelleecctt(2), ppoollll(2), or eeppoollll(7).

BBUUGGSS
